    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3, Chap 831 of 1981",
    "lawSection": "Labor",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7854\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend chapter 831 of the laws of 1981, amending the labor\nlaw relating to fees and expenses in unemployment insurance\nproceedings, in relation to extending the provisions of such chapter\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to extend for two years, until December\n31, 2018, provisions of law relating to fees and expenses in\nunemployment insurance proceedings, which are due to expire on\nDecember 31, 2016.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends Section 3 of Chapter 831 of the Laws of 1981, to\nextend the current sunset date of paragraphs (a) and (c) of\nsubdivision 3 of section 538 of the labor law, from December 31, 2016,\nto December 31, 2018. Paragraphs (a) and (c):\n\n(a) Requires that an agent must provide the Unemployment Insurance\nAppeal Board with their name, business address, and telephone number\nand must also post a $500 bond with the appeal board for the benefit\nof claimants charged certain fees by that agent in order to be\nregistered as authorized to represent claimants.\n\nRequires an agent who seeks to represent an employer to provide the\nAppeal Board with a letter of authorization from the employer in order\nto be registered as authorized.\n\nProhibits the registration of any agent who knowingly violates the law\nregarding compensation of representatives for services rendered to a\nclaimant.\n\n(c) Requires the Appeal Board to maintain lists of authorized agents\nand attorneys who are available to represent claimants and provides\nthat these lists must be made available to claimants upon request.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nChapter 831 of the Laws of 1981 set standards for the fees and\ncompensation of attorneys who represent claimants, as well as fees for\nany witnesses that may be involved, in proceedings before the\nUnemployment Insurance Appeal Board. The expiring provisions also\nestablish guidelines for the agents who are authorized by the Board to\nprovide representation services in these appellate proceedings.  These\nare important consumer protections that ensure that claimants are\nproperly represented and are not charged an undue fee. It is important\nto ensure that as more New York citizens rely on Unemployment\nInsurance while seeking sustainable. employment that they continue to\nbe represented by qualified agents at fair rates.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\n\nNone noted.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
